# Continuity Policy (hard constraint)

Coding tasks in this project often span multiple Cursor sessions — the human
may close Cursor, come back days later, or start a fresh chat mid-task. This
policy makes sure the agent chain can resume **exactly** where it left off —
which workflow, which step/agent, which branch, what's done vs pending —
instead of restarting from scratch or losing context. State lives in
`memory/progress.md`.

## Rules
1. At the very start of any task in any session, read `memory/progress.md`
   (in addition to `AGENTS.md`) before doing anything else. If it shows an
   in-progress task, resume from that exact agent/step — do not restart the
   workflow from Planner unless the recorded step already was Planner, or
   the human explicitly asks to start over.
2. After finishing its own step (at every handoff point in any workflow),
   update `memory/progress.md` with what was just completed, what's next,
   and the current branch name — before ending its turn. This must happen
   even right before a STOP point (e.g. the GitHub push-approval stop),
   since the human may close the session immediately after.
3. If `memory/progress.md` is ambiguous or contradicts the visible repo
   state (e.g. it says "Backend in progress" but that branch doesn't exist,
   or it was already merged), stop and ask the human to reconcile instead of
   guessing.
4. When a feature/bugfix is fully done (PR merged) or a version is fully
   Released, clear/reset the "Active Task" section back to its `<fill in>`
   template state and append a one-line entry to the "Recently Completed"
   trail — keep the Active section clean for the next task.
5. Never let `memory/progress.md` go stale across a handoff — a step that
   forgets to update it is the one thing that breaks resumability for the
   next session.

## Why
- Without this, every new session either re-reads the whole conversation
  history (if it still exists) or the human has to re-explain where things
  stood — both waste time and tokens, and the second one is error-prone.
- This is the task/step-level sibling to two mechanisms that already keep
  other kinds of state small and current:
  - `.cursor/rules/versioning.mdc` — keeps **version-level** state in
    `memory/roadmap.md` (which version is current, what's in/out of scope).
  - `.cursor/rules/graphify.mdc` — keeps **codebase-structure** state in
    `graphify-out/` (what the code looks like, how it connects).
  `memory/progress.md` fills the remaining gap: **where exactly the current
  task/step is**, so a session interrupted mid-Backend-step or right after a
  Reviewer verdict can pick back up without re-deriving anything.
